The mutable default argument `DEFAULT_TAGS` is shared across every call to `make_post`. Each call appends `"post"` to the same list object, so after the first test runs, `DEFAULT_TAGS` becomes `["draft", "post"]`; a second call appends again, producing `["draft", "post", "post"]`. Alone, the test only ever sees the first call, so it passes; in a full suite, an earlier test (or a prior call within the same test file) mutates the shared default before this test runs, so the assertion fails.

Fix it by giving each call its own list instead of reusing one:

```python
def make_post(title, tags=None):
    if tags is None:
        tags = list(DEFAULT_TAGS)
    tags.append("post")
    return {"title": title, "tags": tags}
```

This copies `DEFAULT_TAGS` per call, so mutations never leak between calls or tests.

