The callback loses its `this` binding: `NaN` prints because `this` inside the `function () {...}` passed to `setInterval` isn't the `Timer` instance.

## Cause

A regular `function` gets its own `this`, set by how it's called. `setInterval` calls the callback as a plain function, not as a method on the timer, so inside the callback `this` is `undefined` (class bodies run in strict mode). Reading `this.seconds` then reading works, `undefined += 1` produces `NaN`, and `console.log` prints that `NaN` every tick.

## Fix

Replace the callback with an arrow function. Arrow functions don't have their own `this`; they use the `this` of the enclosing scope, which is the `Timer` instance inside `start()`.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

With this change, `this` inside the callback stays bound to the `Timer` instance, `this.seconds` starts at `0`, and the console prints `1`, `2`, `3`, and so on.
